# Conventional Commits

When creating git commit messages, always use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/).

## Format

```
<type>[optional scope]: <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

- **type**: what kind of change this is (required)
- **scope**: optional area of the codebase (e.g. `control-center`, `library`, `pihole`, `caddy`, `compose`)
- **description**: imperative, lowercase, no trailing period (required)
- **body**: optional; explain what and why, not how
- **footer**: optional; e.g. `BREAKING CHANGE: ...` or `Refs #123`

## Allowed types

| Type | Use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | New user-facing feature |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `style` | Formatting, whitespace, no logic change |
| `refactor` | Code change that is neither a fix nor a feature |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `test` | Adding or updating tests |
| `build` | Build system or dependencies |
| `ci` | CI/CD configuration |
| `chore` | Maintenance, tooling, misc |
| `revert` | Reverting a prior commit |

## Examples

```
feat(library): add install options modal for recipe fields
```

```
fix(immich): use shared modulab network and fix DB bootstrap
```

```
docs(readme): clarify LAN DNS and proxy setup
```

```
chore(cursor): add conventional commits rule
```

## Rules

- Use imperative mood: `add`, `fix`, `update` — not `added`, `fixes`, `updating`
- Keep the subject line ≤ 72 characters
- Do not capitalize the first word after the colon (unless a proper noun)
- If the change is breaking, add a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer or use `feat!` / `fix!`
- Match the scope to the area touched when it aids clarity; omit if unclear
